1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,520
This is SecReqGen, the tool
I built for my MSc thesis.

2
00:00:04,840 --> 00:00:10,420
A user story says what a feature should
do, but often not which parts of the

3
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:12,100
product must protect it.

4
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:17,120
And without the product's documentation,
a language model gives mostly general

5
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:23,360
advice. So SecReqGen searches the
documentation first, then drafts security

6
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:25,700
requirements and a threat list.

7
00:00:25,780 --> 00:00:29,500
Here it is in the browser,
first for security requirements.

8
00:00:29,780 --> 00:00:34,800
I give it a Mattermost user story,
the Mattermost documentation, and a short

9
00:00:34,840 --> 00:00:38,100
description of the system,
then I press Start.

10
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:44,000
It searches the documentation, the model
writes the requirements, and SecReqGen

11
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:45,440
reads the answer.

12
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:51,120
Here is one of them: guests can use
only the channels they were added to.

13
00:00:51,360 --> 00:00:56,020
Priority critical, ASVS
chapter V4, access control.

14
00:00:56,060 --> 00:01:02,140
Its reason cites the passages it used,
and it comes with acceptance criteria.

15
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:08,460
Now the threat list. Same story and
documentation, a writer model and a

16
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:14,267
checker model, and I ask for a threat
actor and a STRIDE category, then press

17
00:01:14,307 --> 00:01:15,141
Start.

18
00:01:14,980 --> 00:01:21,140
The writer drafts threats with quotes,
SecReqGen checks each quote against the

19
00:01:21,180 --> 00:01:26,700
documentation, the writer adds the actor
and the STRIDE category, and the checker

20
00:01:26,740 --> 00:01:29,340
accepts or flags each threat.

21
00:01:29,470 --> 00:01:35,730
Here is one threat: guests find public
channels, or message people outside their

22
00:01:35,770 --> 00:01:39,910
channels. External individual,
information disclosure.

23
00:01:40,290 --> 00:01:43,570
On the right is its quote
from the documentation.

24
00:01:43,770 --> 00:01:46,550
The checker accepted it, so I keep it.
